WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:19.170
&gt;&gt; I just want to start by making what may
sound like a very, very obvious point, but

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:29.440
one that at times can become obscured: that
this is all about people with congenital heart

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:38.380
disease, some very young babies born with
heart problems, and our collective responsibility

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:46.579
to secure the services for them, not just
now but, for when they grow, as adults, and

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:48.670
indeed for children to come.

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:54.570
There are times, when you see some of the
debate over the past, that you might think

00:00:54.570 --> 00:01:04.720
this is simply a competition between different
locations, and I think if we allowed ourselves

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:13.020
to slip into that sort of thinking, we would
be doing a serious disservice to people who

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:14.590
depend upon these services in this country.

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:22.750
This is about those people, their needs and
their continuing needs, now and into the future.

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:29.720
The context for how this has come to the Board
is set out in the paper.

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:36.610
It is a fairly long story, and it is a story
of the Safe and Sustainable process, which

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:44.220
was run under the previous health system through
a joint national committee of Primary Care

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:53.280
Trusts with the intention of securing better
services for -- at that time -- children and

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:58.140
babies with congenital heart problems.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:05.740
The process took four years; it was very intensive
process, but it was challenged in two ways:

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:16.110
one through the courts in judicial review,
and through IRP advice.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:29.049
Now, both of these challenges when they played
out did identify flaws in that process, and

00:02:29.049 --> 00:02:36.239
last month the Secretary of State accepted
that the proposals could not go ahead as planned

00:02:36.239 --> 00:02:43.859
and asked NHS England -- we are now the successor
to that joint committee of PCTs -- to take

00:02:43.859 --> 00:02:48.870
the process forward and to take it forward
in the light of the IRP recommendations.

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:53.129
So, that is why we are discussing it here
today.

00:02:53.129 --> 00:03:00.620
I just want to make a few observations about
the context before we talk a little bit about

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:02.469
the process.

00:03:02.469 --> 00:03:09.499
I think the first really important point is
neither the judicial review process nor indeed

00:03:09.499 --> 00:03:16.290
the independent review panel at all questioned
the need for change in these services.

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:25.269
So, they supported the aspirations to improve
outcomes, improve the sustainability of safe

00:03:25.269 --> 00:03:31.639
services into the future and recognised that
there was some important work that had been

00:03:31.639 --> 00:03:34.010
done with that aim.

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:39.560
A second point is that, as I say, many of
the clinicians and patient groups that we

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:45.650
have managed to speak to since we took on
responsibility have been involved in this

00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:54.139
for many years, living this and eating and
drinking this all the time.

00:03:54.139 --> 00:04:03.269
I think, understandably, they are now questioning
the passion with which we would want to take

00:04:03.269 --> 00:04:10.590
this process forward, so there are some questions
that we will need to address about our determination

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:16.690
to improve outcomes and create absolutely
excellent services in future, given a lot

00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:23.490
of the difficulties that were had with the
previous process.

00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:31.090
I have heard suggestions, again from those
meetings over the past weeks, that one of

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:40.780
the consequences of the review findings has
been weaker relationships between some of

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:48.060
the units currently providing services, and
again I may ask Bruce to comment on this when

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:57.449
I finish the introduction, but it feels to
me that that is quite a dangerous thing.

00:04:57.449 --> 00:05:05.650
This is a service which I think depends upon
the leading clinicians working together, communicating

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:11.920
and cooperating really well, and we need to
make sure that in taking this forward we are

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:18.500
trying to strengthen that collaboration in
the interests of patients and certainly not

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:25.530
doing anything which would weaken or exacerbate
problems that I think are there now following

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:28.560
the current process.

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:34.620
The most hopeful thing that I can share with
you is that whoever I have spoken to, whether

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:42.210
they have felt partially done by either through
the process or through the findings of the

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:49.840
reviews, without exception I have met people
with an absolute passion to improve care and

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:54.139
improve the quality of care for patients.

00:05:54.139 --> 00:05:58.770
Not just now, but what is really remarkable
is that these are people who have lived with

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:05.800
these services for a long time and feel almost
a generational responsibility to be part of

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:10.330
securing even better, even more excellent
services in years to come.

00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:19.490
So, there is that passion there as well as
if you like a question mark over our ability

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:21.199
to really drive ahead.

00:06:21.199 --> 00:06:26.330
So, that is the context, I think, and the
environment that I have picked up from the

00:06:26.330 --> 00:06:28.720
discussions I have had so far.

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:36.419
As I said, the Safe and Sustainable process
took four years, and I do not believe we have

00:06:36.419 --> 00:06:48.530
got that time.

00:06:48.530 --> 00:07:00.569
We are back within twelve months with a plan
that we can implement for improvement and

00:07:00.569 --> 00:07:05.580
change, and that is what is reflected in this
paper.

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:14.050
I am quite sure in the discussions I have
had, so far, that we need to talk much more

00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:15.760
about a national service.

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:28.389
So, this is not a service with very many clinicians,
it is not a huge service in terms of the demand;

00:07:28.389 --> 00:07:34.819
it should be a service that we see as one
serving the whole population, and I think

00:07:34.819 --> 00:07:39.550
that should be the case in the standards we
would expect everywhere, in the collaboration

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:41.889
and the cooperation we expect everywhere.

00:07:41.889 --> 00:07:47.280
Again, that message about a national service
in the public interest is one that so far

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:50.840
has had a strong response from clinicians.

00:07:50.840 --> 00:07:58.490
In the paper itself we set out at paragraph
21 a set of principles which we propose to

00:07:58.490 --> 00:08:05.330
underpin the approach we want to take forward,
as well as setting out in paragraph 23 the

00:08:05.330 --> 00:08:10.889
governance arrangements which will enable
us to make decisions.

00:08:10.889 --> 00:08:16.379
I spoke to a number of the patient groups
just the day before yesterday, and one of

00:08:16.379 --> 00:08:23.150
the points they were making is: try not, if
you can avoid it, to keep all the decisions

00:08:23.150 --> 00:08:24.900
to the end.

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:28.039
I think that is a very good point for us.

00:08:28.039 --> 00:08:37.610
If we find spaces of agreement, whether it's
over standards, whether it's over how networks

00:08:37.610 --> 00:08:44.640
and collaboration should work, we should take
the opportunity to capture those because I

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:48.670
think that will help improve services as we
go along.

00:08:48.670 --> 00:08:56.490
So, that is another principle that I perhaps
suggest we supplement the paper with.

00:08:56.490 --> 00:09:03.640
My final message of introduction, I think,
is to have a degree of confidence.

00:09:03.640 --> 00:09:13.490
I do know people are worried about this, I
do know they feel hard done by, but I think

00:09:13.490 --> 00:09:22.649
we need to display the confidence and the
determination to bring about the right thing

00:09:22.649 --> 00:09:24.089
for patients.

00:09:24.089 --> 00:09:29.029
In that discussion we were having yesterday
about the specialised commissioning and how

00:09:29.029 --> 00:09:34.720
becoming a national organisation and national
commissioner is perhaps enabling us to break

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:40.120
through some of the difficulties that previously
have been experienced, I would say this might

00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:47.830
be a first opportunity to put that into practice
in a albeit controversial area, but nevertheless

00:09:47.830 --> 00:09:51.310
one that is extremely important for patients
and families.

00:09:51.310 --> 00:09:56.160
I do not know if Bruce wanted to add to that?

00:09:56.160 --> 00:10:04.200
&gt;&gt; I would quite like to come at it from a
slightly different perspective, because some

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:10.200
people have asked why we spend quite so much
time focussing on something which is really

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:12.800
a very small specialty.

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:20.920
It is only about 10% of heart surgery in general,
which itself is only a tiny proportion of

00:10:20.920 --> 00:10:31.140
NHS work, but I think it is a small and really
important specialty because, firstly, there

00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:38.089
is nothing more precious than your child and,
secondly, there are no more vulnerable people

00:10:38.089 --> 00:10:45.300
in our society than these children who, if
they get good surgery, can live a successful

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:50.160
and fulfilling life.

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:58.440
One of the problems is that nowhere do we
see such a complex mix of science, technology,

00:10:58.440 --> 00:11:07.180
surgical skill, ethics and human emotion come
together than in this speciality, and against

00:11:07.180 --> 00:11:10.500
that background, the nature of the surgery
is changing.

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:16.769
So, it is quite clear now that the sooner
you do many of these operations, which used

00:11:16.769 --> 00:11:23.889
to be done when children were of a reasonable
size, the better the results you get.

00:11:23.889 --> 00:11:29.030
So, these operations are being conducted on
babies now who are much smaller; their hearts

00:11:29.030 --> 00:11:31.100
are really tiny.

00:11:31.100 --> 00:11:37.220
It requires huge technical skill to be able
to do it, with great emotional demands on

00:11:37.220 --> 00:11:41.470
those doing the operations and those looking
after the children afterwards.

00:11:41.470 --> 00:11:45.399
I am a heart surgeon, as you know, by background;
I could not do this technically.

00:11:45.399 --> 00:11:51.240
It is really complicated and the emotional
side is really demanding.

00:11:51.240 --> 00:11:56.389
Furthermore, there is very complex intensive
care management involved in the recovery of

00:11:56.389 --> 00:12:01.420
children after these complicated operations.

00:12:01.420 --> 00:12:05.800
For such a small specialty, there is quite
a vast array of operations done in different

00:12:05.800 --> 00:12:11.680
combinations, something of the order of 150,
and quite frankly not all surgeons are equally

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:17.680
skilled at doing each and every one of those
operations, but all the surgeons in the country

00:12:17.680 --> 00:12:22.050
need to do enough of some sort of operation
to keep their hands in.

00:12:22.050 --> 00:12:26.899
So, that is not enough.

00:12:26.899 --> 00:12:37.280
Both professional and parental expectations
of a good result are growing, and that poses

00:12:37.280 --> 00:12:40.640
quite a significant demand on the services.

00:12:40.640 --> 00:12:47.579
So, what we have to focus on, and I am quite
clear about this, is getting the best outcomes

00:12:47.579 --> 00:12:52.470
for these babies, because if you do not get
the best outcome it does not just ruin things

00:12:52.470 --> 00:12:55.480
for the baby, it ruins things for the whole
family.

00:12:55.480 --> 00:12:59.550
That is really, in my view, really, really
important.

00:12:59.550 --> 00:13:07.420
So, if we want to get the best outcomes then
we have to have an unequivocal ambition to

00:13:07.420 --> 00:13:13.170
develop what I think could be the best children's
heart surgery service in the world.

00:13:13.170 --> 00:13:17.600
I think that is within our grasp.

00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:24.600
The trouble is, because of the complex mix
in all of this, that the closer we get to

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:31.459
some of the changes, personal and professional
interests and occasionally political interests

00:13:31.459 --> 00:13:39.850
conspire to perpetuate the status quo and
inhibit the pursuit of excellence for these

00:13:39.850 --> 00:13:42.230
really small and vulnerable kids.

00:13:42.230 --> 00:13:48.720
So, I do not want to lose the ambition and
I am sure none of us want to lose the ambition

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:53.620
for this service, not just because I think
it will be really good for our health service

00:13:53.620 --> 00:13:57.470
and really good for the specialty, but most
importantly because it will be really good

00:13:57.470 --> 00:14:01.840
for the kids that are treated.

00:14:01.840 --> 00:14:09.220
The trouble is, as Bill said, for many people
this has ground on for so long that they are

00:14:09.220 --> 00:14:17.449
feeling utterly dispirited and disheartened;
the atmosphere at times has become quite toxic,

00:14:17.449 --> 00:14:23.660
and we have to start to try and get back to
an even playing field without losing much

00:14:23.660 --> 00:14:30.670
of the good stuff that has been done and also
being sure that our evidence base for change

00:14:30.670 --> 00:14:33.199
is absolutely appropriate.

00:14:33.199 --> 00:14:39.839
So, I think there are people out there who
are still up for this ambition, but we owe

00:14:39.839 --> 00:14:46.050
it to them to come to a solution much quicker
than we have tried to in the past, as Bill

00:14:46.050 --> 00:14:47.130
alluded to.

00:14:47.130 --> 00:14:55.649
So, we need further debate with colleagues
and we really need that debate on how we get

00:14:55.649 --> 00:15:03.019
there, because much of the disheartenment
that people feel has come from that they just

00:15:03.019 --> 00:15:04.870
have not felt the process has been right.

00:15:04.870 --> 00:15:07.779
They have not felt that it has been tackled
in the right way.

00:15:07.779 --> 00:15:14.720
So, we need to get agreement on that, I think,
fairly soon.

00:15:14.720 --> 00:15:17.269
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much, Bruce.

00:15:17.269 --> 00:15:20.890
You remind us why we are here: it is the patient.

00:15:20.890 --> 00:15:25.649
It is doing the very best for the patient,
and I think the suggestion that came through

00:15:25.649 --> 00:15:31.810
in Bill's introduction about a national service
which is able to ensure the highest quality

00:15:31.810 --> 00:15:38.880
of treatment, and your points about the multiplicity
of operations and the inability of any surgeon

00:15:38.880 --> 00:15:44.720
to be at the top of the world on more than
a limited number, is absolutely critical.

00:15:44.720 --> 00:15:53.080
So, may I invite any further comments as to
the paper and the objectives of the next phase

00:15:53.080 --> 00:15:56.810
of the review and the process that we propose
to undertake?

00:15:56.810 --> 00:16:15.709
No; I think generally people are warmly behind
this, Bill, so thank you very much.

00:16:15.709 --> 00:16:23.509
&gt;&gt; This is the independent report that the
Board commissioned to ensure that we can learn

00:16:23.509 --> 00:16:29.980
the lessons for handling major projects, drawing
form the experience of the introduction of

00:16:29.980 --> 00:16:37.620
NHS 111 which, as the Board knows, we took
responsibility for from 1st April but which

00:16:37.620 --> 00:16:40.430
had a history which stretched back.

00:16:40.430 --> 00:16:49.579
So, while the review has concentrated on lessons
we can learn, it has of course gone back further

00:16:49.579 --> 00:16:51.120
than just the lifetime of NHS England.

00:16:51.120 --> 00:17:00.129
I am very grateful to Peter Garland, who conducted
the review for us.

00:17:00.129 --> 00:17:07.750
Peter was a Regional Director of the NHS in
the north a number of years ago, but has retired

00:17:07.750 --> 00:17:12.660
and was completely independent and insisted
upon being completely independent in drawing

00:17:12.660 --> 00:17:15.310
together these lessons.

00:17:15.310 --> 00:17:22.100
I think there are some clear messages going
forward and they are set out in summary in

00:17:22.100 --> 00:17:29.820
paragraph four; I will not go through them,
Chairman, other than to reassure you that

00:17:29.820 --> 00:17:36.220
the proposed actions in paragraph five for
NHS England, going forward, are intended to

00:17:36.220 --> 00:17:37.940
address them.

00:17:37.940 --> 00:17:45.360
They are about the professionalism of our
due diligence when we are commissioning very

00:17:45.360 --> 00:17:54.270
significant contracts and projects like this;
they are about the granularity of understanding

00:17:54.270 --> 00:18:00.520
the risks and then the ability to raise those
risks to the right place swiftly and with

00:18:00.520 --> 00:18:04.220
confidence that they will be heard and acted
upon.

00:18:04.220 --> 00:18:09.780
I think they are importantly about senior
oversight and, indeed, we are recommending

00:18:09.780 --> 00:18:17.020
that we have a major projects oversight group
as part of the way we oversee risks for the

00:18:17.020 --> 00:18:26.350
organisation, and I think quite a significant
message here is the importance of piloting

00:18:26.350 --> 00:18:27.650
an evaluation.

00:18:27.650 --> 00:18:34.240
So, where you can with a very significant
large scale project, we should be trying to

00:18:34.240 --> 00:18:39.690
introduce almost piloting and evaluating as
you go along.

00:18:39.690 --> 00:18:47.920
The importance of evaluation, which connects
absolutely to our values about being evidence-based

00:18:47.920 --> 00:18:53.290
and rigorous, I think is a very important
one.

00:18:53.290 --> 00:19:02.300
We have had a first sight of which sort of
projects going forward should be caught by

00:19:02.300 --> 00:19:10.700
this, and I know we have suggested for starters
three: first, NHS 111, and I know Barbara

00:19:10.700 --> 00:19:17.980
may want to comment at some point on what
has already been done there; the transition

00:19:17.980 --> 00:19:26.610
of the family health service to the administration
service for primary care, which we are committed

00:19:26.610 --> 00:19:33.320
to doing in our business plan; and also our
interface with customers through the customer

00:19:33.320 --> 00:19:41.909
service platform and the contact centre, where
we think there is opportunity to do an awful

00:19:41.909 --> 00:19:47.520
lot better into the future than we have achieved
in the past in the NHS, but which will be

00:19:47.520 --> 00:19:49.320
a major undertaking.

00:19:49.320 --> 00:19:55.230
There will be others, and I would suggest
that on the back of this we share with colleagues

00:19:55.230 --> 00:20:01.630
a list of potential major projects and certainly
Ed in his Audit Committee role to cast an

00:20:01.630 --> 00:20:03.450
eye on some of that.

00:20:03.450 --> 00:20:19.729
So, the recommendation is to receive the report
and to consider the action plan proposed.

00:20:19.729 --> 00:20:27.440
&gt;&gt; For the avoidance of doubt, Peter Garland's
report is made public because that is our

00:20:27.440 --> 00:20:28.440
commitment.

00:20:28.440 --> 00:20:35.330
He was very rigorous in his review; in a way,
there is a bit of a hospital pass somewhere

00:20:35.330 --> 00:20:38.990
hidden in this, and a second hospital pass
to Barbara who picked it up on 1st April and

00:20:38.990 --> 00:20:46.289
has done a splendid job in managing the system
for stability.

00:20:46.289 --> 00:20:48.190
None of this is rocket science.

00:20:48.190 --> 00:20:53.820
This is the real difficulty with this; when
you see these reports, you go, 'But yes, this

00:20:53.820 --> 00:20:55.029
is obvious'.

00:20:55.029 --> 00:21:01.419
The key thing to me is that we create institutional
learning from this incident, which I think

00:21:01.419 --> 00:21:07.270
we are absolutely committed to doing, that
we create the governance and we create the

00:21:07.270 --> 00:21:11.480
capacity to get these things done and we do
not rush at them.

00:21:11.480 --> 00:21:18.200
The end to end cost of rectifying something
that goes at speed without sufficient thought

00:21:18.200 --> 00:21:27.090
and without sufficient testing is significantly
higher than the end to end cost of getting

00:21:27.090 --> 00:21:31.990
it right by staging it and working through
it in the proper programme project management

00:21:31.990 --> 00:21:34.900
way with appropriate oversight.

00:21:34.900 --> 00:21:40.289
I think if there is one great thing that comes
out of this for us, it is how to do these

00:21:40.289 --> 00:21:44.790
with those key major projects that we have
in the future.

00:21:44.790 --> 00:21:53.260
&gt;&gt; Yes, and just to reassure the Board that
immediately, at the end of March, most of

00:21:53.260 --> 00:21:55.640
these recommendations were put in place, certainly.

00:21:55.640 --> 00:22:02.590
Initially, I think it was about serious attention
being given to this by our senior managers

00:22:02.590 --> 00:22:07.360
and senior clinicians across the country in
terms of supporting CCGs, so our regions and

00:22:07.360 --> 00:22:10.610
areas have done a sterling job on that.

00:22:10.610 --> 00:22:16.179
It was about securing additional resource
in the national support centre dedicated to

00:22:16.179 --> 00:22:21.630
this, but most of all I think putting in place
a programme oversight group with proper rigorous

00:22:21.630 --> 00:22:26.740
programme management assurance and risk mitigation
process.

00:22:26.740 --> 00:22:34.360
As I said, that the programme oversight group
meets regularly and in accordance with our

00:22:34.360 --> 00:22:40.460
assurance of risk processes, a review of what
is going on in 111 is frequently taken to

00:22:40.460 --> 00:22:44.060
the Executive team who talk about this and
summarily to the Board.

00:22:44.060 --> 00:22:50.810
Another couple of areas which reflect what
is in the report as well: it suggests flexibility

00:22:50.810 --> 00:22:55.470
on time, and I reported earlier that we were
flexible on the rollout of 111 in a couple

00:22:55.470 --> 00:22:56.490
of areas.

00:22:56.490 --> 00:23:04.929
Also, in terms of assurance and checks, we
are expecting any new provider of 111 to undertake

00:23:04.929 --> 00:23:11.080
due diligence and share this with us, but
we are also ensuring that we have a full external

00:23:11.080 --> 00:23:16.890
assurance process available to our CCGs to
look at any new providers, so that will be

00:23:16.890 --> 00:23:18.170
a dual process.

00:23:18.170 --> 00:23:25.029
Finally, in terms of procurement, Tim and
I are ensuring that we find a way to have

00:23:25.029 --> 00:23:30.860
a national procurement expertise which can
support CCGs when they undertake any of these

00:23:30.860 --> 00:23:32.529
similar procurements.

00:23:32.529 --> 00:23:41.990
&gt;&gt; I think it is really helpful that this
comes so early in our existence, and I would

00:23:41.990 --> 00:23:46.740
just like to suggest maybe Bill and I pick
up an action which is to do some systematic

00:23:46.740 --> 00:23:50.760
staff briefing with the staff involved in
some of the projects we have already identified,

00:23:50.760 --> 00:23:55.490
but then maybe identifying some training needs
falling out of that that we can roll out across

00:23:55.490 --> 00:23:59.190
the organisation.

00:23:59.190 --> 00:24:05.140
&gt;&gt; I do think, going back to the conversation
we had about the mandate, is this issue about

00:24:05.140 --> 00:24:09.600
accountability and who is accountable for
what is really important on this.

00:24:09.600 --> 00:24:15.030
Part of the issue here was the department;
ourselves and CCGs.

00:24:15.030 --> 00:24:20.520
There are other examples, proton beam therapy
and others.

00:24:20.520 --> 00:24:24.970
This forces you to get that clarity of accountability,
and where you get it confused, that is where

00:24:24.970 --> 00:24:26.970
we get problems, I think.

00:24:26.970 --> 00:24:29.970
&gt;&gt; Thank you and I agree with all of that.

00:24:29.970 --> 00:24:36.850
I think the critical thing is that when something
goes wrong we do not overlook it or cover

00:24:36.850 --> 00:24:39.980
it up, but actually we get on and find out
what went wrong and we learn from it.

00:24:39.980 --> 00:24:46.870
I am really pleased; I thought that this report
was clear and has enormous opportunities for

00:24:46.870 --> 00:24:47.870
learning.

00:24:47.870 --> 00:24:51.230
It is not a blame-casting report; we are not
into that game.

00:24:51.230 --> 00:24:57.580
Our whole future depends upon our understanding
properly how we conduct major projects for

00:24:57.580 --> 00:24:59.669
the future and how we learn from each one
that we do.

00:24:59.669 --> 00:25:07.500
I am very grateful to you, Ed, for initiating
with Paul the review that has been undertaken.

00:25:07.500 --> 00:25:08.679
Thank you.

00:25:08.679 --> 00:25:19.210
&gt;&gt; One point, if I may: in your paper, Bill,
paragraph nine I find is a bit too passive

00:25:19.210 --> 00:25:21.289
about reporting to the Board.

00:25:21.289 --> 00:25:25.600
So, it says, 'Where there are areas of risk
that need to be highlighted to the Board,

00:25:25.600 --> 00:25:28.270
these can be escalated.'

00:25:28.270 --> 00:25:31.760
I would put it in a more positive tone.

00:25:31.760 --> 00:25:38.940
So, if you go back to the 111 experience,
one of the things I would say is the Board

00:25:38.940 --> 00:25:44.460
had no visibility on this being a looming
problem, and I think we should put a higher

00:25:44.460 --> 00:25:47.130
onus on reporting to the Board.

00:25:47.130 --> 00:25:50.200
&gt;&gt; You are happy with that Bill?

00:25:50.200 --> 00:25:51.200
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:25:51.200 --> 00:25:52.200
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:25:52.200 --> 00:25:52.840
Thank you very much for the report.

